  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Friday)\nIssued at 1236 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nA mild day is underway across the Mid-South. There is quite a bit\nmore cloud cover than expected along and east of the MS River,\nwhich is slowing the temperature climb a bit. However, the clouds\nshould continue to spread NW and scatter out with highs reaching\nthe lower to mid 70s areawide this afternoon.\n\nA weakening mid-level shortwave will exit the Southern Rockies\nthis evening and push a cold front toward the Mid-South on\nSunday. Moisture will be drawn northward in advance of the front,\nthough weak low-level flow will result in dewpoints struggling to\nreach 60F across parts of the Delta south and west of Memphis,\nwhere the 12z REFS has about 500 J/kg of SBCAPE Sunday afternoon.\nMarginal instability combined with weakening deep-layer shear,\npoor mid-level lapse rates, and weak low-level flow will limit\nchances of strong to severe storms. There is a slight chance (15-\n20%) of thunder along and west of the MS River during the\nafternoon hours. Since this system is on a general weakening\ntrend as it moves through with weak moisture advections and\nwaning upper support, QPF amounts will be light, generally under\none-quarter inch, despite PWATs around the 90th percentile.\n\nThe cold front will exit the region Sunday night with cool and\ndry high pressure settling over the region for the early part of\nnext week. Temperatures will drop below normal with gusty north\nwinds, making it feel like late fall. Cold morning lows will\nreturn by early Tuesday, when temperatures dip into the upper 20s\nand lower 30s despite a northerly breeze.\n\nThe middle of the week is starting to look somewhat interesting,\nand forecast confidence is decreasing. A westwardly moving\ninverted trough is expected to reach AL by Wednesday morning.\nMeanwhile, a potent piece of energy is forecast to move into the\nLower MS Valley by Wednesday morning. As this upper feature\ninteracts with the inverted trough, rain will develop. Exactly\nwhere this rain develops will depend on the location of the\ninverted trough and where the interaction occurs. The LREF puts\nmost of the rain in AL and Middle TN, with areas along the TN\nRiver Valley receiving perhaps up to one-quarter inch on\nWednesday. This amount could increase and shift west if the\ninverted trough makes it further west, say into NE MS, as\ndepicted by the 12z GFS.\n\nThat system will quickly move northeast and provide fuel for a\nholiday nor'easter along the Atlantic Seaboard. Meanwhile, cool\nhigh pressure will build into the region. Clouds will likely\nlinger on Thanksgiving in the wake of the departing system.\nTemperatures will be seasonably cool for the holiday into early\nnext weekend, with highs in the 50s and lows in the 20s and 30s.",
